# Deny everything by default; the image build only needs the Go sources.
# This keeps secrets (.env), per-request dumps and harness output (logs/ can
# reach tens of GB), compiled binaries, tests, and docs out of the build context — and
# guards against future files accidentally leaking into the image.
*

# Allow only what's required to compile the two binaries (plus the LICENSE,
# which ships in the image): the root Go sources and the single testupstream
# source. Re-include the directory so Docker descends into it, then re-exclude
# its contents and allow only main.go — so a future file dropped under
# testupstream/ can't silently enter the context.
!go.mod
!VERSION
!LICENSE
!*.go
!testupstream/
testupstream/*
!testupstream/main.go

# Copy only enough Git metadata for Dockerfile to stamp the binary with the
# current commit. Do not include .git/config, hooks, objects, or logs — do NOT
# add a bare `!.git/` here: it re-includes the whole directory.
!.git/HEAD
!.git/refs/
!.git/refs/**
!.git/packed-refs

# ...but still drop test sources that slipped through the *.go allow above
# (go build ignores them anyway; no reason to send them to the daemon).
*_test.go
